{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790400353,"nonce":"eSuSDKkjlmGi4oGdbPGfMFc9Xkg=","title":"Lua: several results across an embedder","message":"A Lua function's results are as many as its caller keeps; an embedder calling from a language without multiple results (caribou: Haxe, Wren) cannot say at the call site how many it wants, so the function's declared results decide. Two pieces sit in zyntax:\n\n1. The `@return` annotations keep each result's name (`---@return integer count, string label`), so an embedder can hand several results over as a named record (Haxe's anonymous structure). Exports' Signature.returns carries Returned { ty, name }.\n\n2. The other way: a foreign call whose embedder gives back several values (a Python function returning a tuple, a caribou tuple) should give Lua that many results, `local a, b = py.f()`, as a C function returning several does. Today Foreign::call returns one value and a foreign call site keeps it as one.","files":null}]}